# Rust
target/
# Cargo.lock IS tracked (issue #2870): the workspace ships binaries
# (cqlite-flight, cqlite-cli) and the flight image build resolves --locked, so a
# committed lockfile is the precondition that makes the cargo-chef cook layer's
# cache key stable and reproducible. Do NOT re-ignore it.
# The fuzz crate (fuzz/Cargo.toml) is its OWN separate workspace (issue #1614),
# excluded from the main one and built only under nightly by its own workflow —
# its lockfile is not part of any shipped image build, so keep it untracked.
fuzz/Cargo.lock
# MEASUREMENT-HARNESS LOCKFILES ARE TRACKED, and the reasoning that once ignored
# them is kept here because the trade is real and someone will re-propose it.
#
# The case FOR ignoring: roborev appends a COMPILED-IN deny-list (`**/Cargo.lock`,
# `**/go.sum`, ...) that no configuration switches off (#3278), so a committed
# lockfile under `docs/reports/*-artifacts/**` is silently dropped from the
# reviewer's diff. That is real, and it is why `**/Cargo.lock` was ignored here
# and the closure committed under a reviewable alias instead.
#
# WHY THAT WAS THE WRONG WAY ROUND. `=`-exact DIRECT pins do not pin TRANSITIVE
# versions, and a lockfile stored under a name cargo does not read pins nothing
# at all — `cargo build` still resolves a fresh graph, so the binary that produced
# the published numbers was not reproducible. The two costs are not comparable:
# an excluded lockfile costs NOISE (one unreviewed generated file, whose content
# no reviewer would read line-by-line anyway), while an unpinned measured binary
# costs REPRODUCIBILITY IN THE DELIVERABLE ITSELF. Reproducibility wins. The
# lockfile is committed, `sweep.sh` builds `--locked` so a drifted lockfile FAILS
# rather than silently resolving, and the `=`-exact direct pins stay as belt and
# braces.
# Python bytecode from the measurement harnesses' own tooling (guards.py is
# imported by derive.py, so CPython writes one). Generated, never source.
docs/reports/*-artifacts/**/__pycache__/
*.pdb

